.kilo
CLAUDE.md

# A project's varlock schema is tracked; a project's values are not.
# The schema declares names, types and example values, and never a value, so it is
# safe in git and required to be there. Everything else under `.env` is not.
# The negation is UNANCHORED on purpose: `.guarden/.env.schema` contains a slash and
# would match only at the root, silently dropping the schema in every fixture
# repository under fixtures/projects/.
.env
.env.*
!**/.guarden/.env.schema
# The root contract is anchored, unlike the rule above it: `/.env.schema` names
# the one at the top and leaves every nested schema to `!**/.guarden/.env.schema`.
# Unanchored it would also re-admit the fixture repositories', quietly making
# the rule above it dead — and a second rule that never fires is a rule nobody
# can tell is broken.
!/.env.schema

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
*.egg-info/
.ruff_cache/
*.tar.gz
*.dump
evidence/
.lxc-state/

# A rendered cloud-init document is a build output, not a source. What a change
# is made to is the template under `src/guarden/templates/cloud-init/` and
# `packages/packages.toml`; `soil packages --render-user-data` produces the
# document from them. A committed render is a second copy of the package list
# that goes stale the moment the list changes, so no render is tracked and this
# pattern is the shape a render lands in — `user-data.yaml`, `user-data.vps.yaml`,
# `guarden-user-data.yaml`, and whatever a caller names with `--output`.
*user-data*.yaml
